= Mental simulation — Technical =

'''Mental simulation''' comprises cognitive processes by which people construct, manipulate or evaluate representations of possible events without directly experiencing those events at the moment. In psychology, the term covers episodic future thought, counterfactual thinking, motor/action rehearsal and simulation-based judgment heuristics; these are overlapping but nonidentical research traditions. A claim about the neural basis of remembering an autobiographical event does not automatically validate a model of counterfactual causal inference, and an individual's subjective vividness does not establish the truth or probability of a simulated event.

The original simulation-heuristic discussion by Kahneman and Tversky (1982) concerns the ease of constructing examples and causal alternatives as a cue in judgment. Kahneman and Miller's norm theory (1986) develops the role of mentally generated alternatives and normality in emotional and causal responses. Schacter, Addis and Buckner (2007) examine the overlap of episodic retrieval and future-event construction in neuroscience.<ref name="heuristic">Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, [https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/judgment-under-uncertainty/simulation-heuristic/9C6ED91A6429A8443641641DC41A52FE “The Simulation Heuristic”], in ''Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases'' (1982).</ref><ref name="norm">Daniel Kahneman and Dale T. Miller, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-295X.93.2.136 “Norm Theory: Comparing Reality to Its Alternatives”], ''Psychological Review'' 93(2), 136–153 (1986).</ref><ref name="brain">Daniel L. Schacter, Donna Rose Addis and Randy L. Buckner, [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn2213 “Remembering the Past to Imagine the Future: The Prospective Brain”], ''Nature Reviews Neuroscience'' 8, 657–661 (2007).</ref>

== Distinct constructs and measurements ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Construct !! Typical observable !! Important confound or gap
|-
| Episodic future simulation || Number, vividness and internal consistency of generated future-event details || Vividness depends on language, imagery and memory, not just prediction
|-
| Counterfactual simulation || Mentally modified antecedents and corresponding imagined outcomes || Imagined causal dependence does not identify an intervention effect
|-
| Simulation heuristic || Estimated likelihood or reaction as a function of scenario-generation ease || Accessibility can depend on salience, instructions and experimenter demand
|-
| Motor imagery / rehearsal || Subsequent task performance and neural/behavioral correlates || Improvement can depend on actual practice, baseline and skill
|-
| Prospective planning || Prepared actions, error rates, goal attainment || Verbal plans may not transfer into performance under time or resource constraints
|}

The most important measurement question is '''what is being simulated''' and '''what is the validation target'''? It is insufficient to show that a participant can describe possible events. A useful experiment must measure whether simulations alter choices, produce accurate forecasts, facilitate action or systematically distort judgment.

== Counterfactual structure and causal interpretation ==

A counterfactual question changes one or more antecedent conditions while imagining alternative consequences. In a simple structural causal model, one can write

<math display="block">Y=f_Y(X,U),</math>

where <math>X</math> is a candidate intervenable variable and <math>U</math> summarizes other causes. An interventional contrast such as

<math display="block">E[Y\mid\operatorname{do}(X=x_1)]-E[Y\mid\operatorname{do}(X=x_0)]</math>

is a statement about a specified external causal model and intervention semantics. A person's imagined alternative <math>\widetilde Y(x)</math> is instead a report or computational output generated by their own internal model. It does not equal the causal quantity without evidence that their representation accurately captures the target mechanisms and exogenous factors.

For retrospective reasoning, alternative antecedents are constrained by what could realistically have changed. “If the weather had differed” may require modifying multiple environmental influences rather than one isolated observation. Kahneman and Miller's norm theory considers which aspects of a realized event are more likely to be mentally “undone,” and how that affects judgments and affect.<ref name="norm"/>

Counterfactual simulation can be normatively productive for diagnosing failure modes and planning prevention, but it can also encourage hindsight bias or emotionally compelling but untested causal stories. Experimental instructions that change the availability of alternatives can influence measured outcomes without demonstrating that an alternative would really have occurred.

== Episodic construction, memory and neuroscience ==

Episodic future thinking often recombines remembered people, settings and actions into novel scenarios. Neuropsychological and neuroimaging studies identify overlap in systems involved in remembering and imagining, but interpreting overlap requires care: shared activation may reflect common component processes such as scene construction, self-referential thought or retrieval rather than complete functional identity.<ref name="brain"/>

A neural activation result is not itself a measurement of the probability of the imagined future event. Differences in participant age, imagery ability, autobiographical memory and clinical or neurological status can change task performance, making the population and task definition central to generalization.

If an experiment asks participants to imagine a specific event and later measures recalled details, the experiment cannot infer general future-prediction accuracy without an objective outcome benchmark. Reports of subjective vividness and narrative coherence may be useful psychological data in their own right, but they are not external event verification.

== Simulation as a heuristic of probability judgment ==

The simulation heuristic hypothesizes that the ease with which a scenario is imagined can influence judgments about its plausibility or normality. Ease is an observable only indirectly, through response time, ratings, scenario counts or other operational measures. A researcher should distinguish the manipulated ease of construction from frequency and base-rate information.

One can express a limited hypothesis as <math>\widehat p_{\mathrm{judged}}=g(E,B,\theta)</math>, where <math>E</math> denotes ease of generating a scenario, <math>B</math> includes known base-rate information and <math>\theta</math> denotes subject/context factors. This function is a research placeholder, not an established psychophysical law. It should not be assigned a universal linear coefficient without data.

Cognitive strategies can be adaptive in some ecological regimes: frequently occurring events may be easier to recall or imagine. But novel media, salience, emotional importance and repeated prompts can decouple mental availability from real probability. Correct assessment requires comparing judgments with objective frequencies or well-calibrated event probabilities.

== Fully specified probability-calibration illustration ==

To distinguish a mental prediction from an actual event model, imagine a person assigns probability <math>q=0.7</math> to a future binary event. Suppose an independent, explicitly specified external statistical model assigns true event probability <math>p=0.3</math>. A single outcome <math>Y\in\{0,1\}</math> cannot tell whether the judgment was consistently calibrated, but proper-scoring rules can evaluate repeated predictions under a specified reference distribution.

For the binary Brier score <math>BS=(q-Y)^2</math>, expectation under Bernoulli <math>p</math> is

<math display="block">E[BS]=p(1-q)^2+(1-p)q^2=p(1-p)+(q-p)^2.</math>

Substituting <math>p=0.3</math>, <math>q=0.7</math> gives

<math display="block">E[BS]=0.3(0.3)^2+0.7(0.7)^2=0.027+0.343=0.370.</math>

A calibrated forecast <math>q=p=0.3</math> yields expected score <math>0.3(0.7)^2+0.7(0.3)^2=0.210</math>. The additional expected error is <math>(0.7-0.3)^2=0.160</math>. These are conditional mathematical results for an invented probability model; they do not quantify a published simulation-heuristic effect.

If an investigator instead tries to estimate event frequency by drawing <math>N</math> independent events from a correct computer simulator, the sample fraction <math>\hat p=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^N\mathbf1(E_i)</math> has approximate standard error <math>\sqrt{p(1-p)/N}</math>. For <math>p=.3,N=100</math>, that is about 0.0458. However, human mental scenarios are not necessarily independent random samples or draws from a calibrated model; applying that standard error to subjective simulations without validation is invalid.

The example's lesson is methodological: the accuracy of a mental scenario can only be assessed against a defensible target distribution, and formal scoring of imagined probabilities does not establish an accurate causal mechanism.

== Prospective rehearsal, coping and performance ==

S. E. Taylor and S. K. Schneider examined how mental simulation of past, future and hypothetical events may relate to coping and action. Their analysis belongs to a broader literature in which imagined rehearsal sometimes supports preparation, yet content, task, participant characteristics and actual behavioral follow-through all matter.<ref name="coping">Shelley E. Taylor and Sherry K. Schneider, [https://doi.org/10.1521/soco.1989.7.2.174 “Coping and the Simulation of Events”], ''Social Cognition'' 7(2), 174–194 (1989).</ref>

A controlled evaluation might randomly assign a rehearsal protocol, an alternative cognitive exercise and a neutral control. Pre-register primary task outcomes, retention and uncertainty, rather than relying only on confidence or feelings of preparedness. Verbal reports can be affected by demand characteristics; outcome assessment should be blinded where possible.

An experiment on rehearsal and motor execution is not automatic evidence for simulation-heuristic effects on probability estimation, and vice versa. The similar word “simulation” cannot replace a specification of actual tasks and interventions.

== Limitations of introspection and demand effects ==

Participants can report whether an imagined event felt vivid or easy, but introspection may not reveal all underlying computations. Task instructions may affect participants' goals, attention and reported ease. They may generate familiar scenarios rather than likely scenarios; rare dramatic events can be easier to simulate than commonplace low-salience processes.

Cross-cultural and developmental differences can affect narrative detail, counterfactual norms, personal memory and the interpretation of probability scales. A calibrated prediction requires a reference distribution; without one, a study supports only claims about subjective judgment, not objective foresight.

Confounding between scenario construction and language competence is especially important when comparing participant groups or assigning standardized tests. The number of details generated can be influenced by verbal fluency even when some nonverbal simulation capacity is unchanged.

== Artificial agents and computational simulation ==

A computational agent may explicitly sample candidate future states or evaluate rollouts under a transition model:

<math display="block">\hat s_{t+1}\sim P_\theta(s_{t+1}\mid s_t,a_t).</math>

A planner can compare hypothetical actions by estimating downstream outcomes, but its correctness depends on transition-model calibration, reward specification, horizon length, state estimation and distribution shift. That engineering process is not direct evidence of human-like autobiographical mental simulation.

The relevant analogy is methodological: both systems can represent alternatives, and both can be evaluated against outcomes. Claims about shared cognition, subjective experience or neural equivalence require additional evidence that is not contained in the word “simulation.”

== Reproducibility and auditing ==

A rigorous research report should state exactly which simulation construct is examined, define experimental conditions and stimuli, characterize participant sampling, distinguish subjective ratings from externally measured outcomes, check baseline rates, quantify prediction error, use independent validation where appropriate and disclose alternative explanations. For neuroscience, report acquisition and preprocessing and distinguish correlational activation from causal necessity.

The Brier-score fixture above is an independent algebraic verification case: expected score 0.370 for <math>q=.7,p=.3</math> and 0.210 for an optimally calibrated <math>q=.3</math>. Neither result comes from a study of human participants. The scientific claim must rest on genuine experimental data and suitable controls, not on the elegance of the toy equation.

Mental simulation is a legitimate interdisciplinary research area, but not a universal cognitive engine from which all planning, imagination or judgment can be deduced. A good article preserves each empirically defined phenomenon and its limits.

